1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,120
Okay, moving on to epithelial tumors.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:05,060
The first disorder is seborrheic keratosis.

3
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:08,500
This is a benign proliferation of squamous cells.

4
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,020
It commonly arises in the elderly.

5
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:14,560
On gross exam, you see a raised discolored plaque,

6
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:16,440
usually on the extremities or the face,

7
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:17,880
and this is what it looks like.

8
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:21,520
And it has a classic, quote unquote, stuck on appearance.

9
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:22,940
So that when you look at this lesion,

10
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:25,140
it looks like a coin that you can just peel away

11
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:26,960
from the surface of the skin.

12
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:28,780
Were you to examine this on histology,

13
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:30,400
you would see a raised lesion.

14
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,100
This line represents sort of the surface of the skin,

15
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:35,520
and this represents the bump on the skin.

16
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:38,400
And one of the hallmarks is that it contains

17
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:41,900
these circular spaces with abundant pink keratin.

18
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:44,540
And these circular spaces with abundant pink keratin

19
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:46,240
are called pseudocysts.

20
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:49,540
So again, you have a proliferation of the squamous cells

21
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:54,620
with the pseudocysts producing this coin-like stuck-on lesion

22
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:56,260
on the surface of the skin.

23
00:00:56,260 --> 00:01:00,580
Now, seborrheic keratoses are normal in individuals as they age.

24
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:03,000
However, it is possible to have the sudden onset

25
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,480
of multiple seborrheic keratoses,

26
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:08,300
like dozens and dozens of seborrheic keratoses.

27
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:11,820
This is called the lesser Trelot sign, particularly high yield,

28
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:16,160
and it suggests an underlying carcinoma, usually of the GI tract.

29
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:19,080
And so it's probably a paraneoplastic syndrome.

30
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:21,300
But you do need to know, for the purposes of boards,

31
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:24,340
that if you get multiple seborrheic keratoses all of a sudden,

32
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:27,580
think about a carcinoma, especially in the GI tract.

33
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:31,040
Here's a picture just showing you multiple seborrheic keratoses

34
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,540
that have arisen all of a sudden,

35
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:35,360
again hinting that there may be an underlying carcinoma

36
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:37,180
of the GI tract.

37
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:38,620
Acanthosis nigricans.

38
00:01:38,620 --> 00:01:42,340
Remember the word acanthosis means a thick epidermis.

39
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:45,020
And nigricans implies that it becomes dark.

40
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:48,720
So you get epidermal hyperplasia with darkening of the skin.

41
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:52,320
The epidermal hyperplasia makes the skin feel velvet-like.

42
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:54,880
And so again, it becomes a little thicker than normal.

43
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:57,200
This usually occurs in the groin or axilla

44
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:59,900
and is associated with insulin resistance.

45
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:02,840
So for example, type two diabetics can get it,

46
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:04,340
or malignancy.

47
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:06,880
For example, patients with gastric carcinoma

48
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:09,360
can develop acanthosis nigricans,

49
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:11,300
and that's particularly high yield.

50
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:14,000
Here's a clinical picture of acanthosis nigricans.

51
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,440
You can't really appreciate the thickening of the skin,

52
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:20,280
but I think you can appreciate that it is darker than normal.

53
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:23,000
Basal cell carcinoma is a malignant proliferation

54
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,400
of basal cells of the epidermis,

55
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:27,600
and the risk factors are all based on exposure

56
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:29,500
to UVB sunlight.

57
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,200
Now remember that within sunlight,

58
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:34,480
UVB is the most carcinogenic component,

59
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:36,880
and UVB can induce DNA damage.

60
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:39,100
And so when there's UVB-induced DNA damage,

61
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:41,920
that would increase the risk for basal cell carcinoma.

62
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:44,360
So the risk factors are all going to be based on UVB.

63
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:48,480
For example, prolonged exposure to sunlight, excess UVB.

64
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:50,920
Albinism, where you don't have pigment to the skin,

65
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:53,320
so you're not protected against UVB.

66
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:56,680
And finally, a disorder called xeroderma pigmentosum,

67
00:02:56,680 --> 00:02:58,360
which is particularly high yield.

68
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:02,520
Now in xeroderma pigmentosum, you have an autosomal recessive defect

69
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:06,800
in enzymes that are necessary for nucleotide excision repair.

70
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:11,320
Now let's go back and remind ourselves that when sunlight hits DNA in a cell,

71
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:14,200
it causes formation of pyrimidine dimers.

72
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:20,360
These pyrimidine dimers are normally excised by something called the nucleotide excision repair pathway.

73
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:26,800
The enzymes of the nucleotide excision repair pathway remove the pyrimidine dimers and repair that defect.

74
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:31,920
Now imagine if a patient has a defect in the ability to repair these pyrimidine dimers,

75
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:34,800
then there's going to be an increased risk in basal cell carcinoma.

76
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:38,800
And so that's exactly what happens in xeroderma pigmentosum.

77
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:41,800
The patients do not have their nucleotide excision repair pathway,

78
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:47,760
and therefore they get pyrimidine dimers increasing their risk for basal cell carcinoma.

79
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:51,560
Basal cell carcinoma has a very classic appearance clinically.

80
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:59,040
You get this elevated nodule here with a central area of ulceration here,

81
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:04,560
and it's surrounded by all of these dilated blood vessels which are called telangiectasias.

82
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:08,760
And so this is the classic appearance of basal cell carcinoma.

83
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:11,240
It's also important to note for the purposes of board exams

84
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:16,080
that one of the classic locations that basal cell carcinoma can arise in is the upper lip.

85
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:20,480
They like you to know that basal cell carcinoma arises on the upper lip.

86
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:23,280
Here's a picture of basal cell carcinoma on histology.

87
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:25,480
Now you get these nodules here.

88
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:27,200
This whole thing represents tumor.

89
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:29,080
There's also tumor here and here.

90
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:34,600
You get these nodules of basal cells which are characterized by peripheral palisading.

91
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:40,080
Now when you say the word palisading, what you mean is that the cells are lining up against some space.

92
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:45,920
So can you notice here that this particular layer of cells looks a little bit darker than the rest of the cells?

93
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:51,720
And that's because this layer is lining up against the edge, and that's called peripheral palisading.

94
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:55,920
And that's a classic finding on histology of basal cell carcinoma.

95
00:04:55,920 --> 00:05:00,920
The treatment for basal cell carcinoma is surgical excision, and it very rarely metastasizes.

96
00:05:00,920 --> 00:05:03,760
It has an excellent prognosis.

97
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:07,760
Squamous cell carcinoma is a malignant proliferation of squamous cells.

98
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:15,760
The risk factors are all based on exposure to UVB sunlight, which can then induce DNA damage by forming pyrimidine dimers.

99
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:26,760
So of course, exposure to sunlight would be a risk factor, albinism would be a risk factor, and zero dermapigmentosin when you don't have the enzymes necessary to repair those pyrimidine dimers.

100
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:32,920
Additional risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma are very high yield for the board exam, so I threw them in here.

101
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:35,400
The first is immunosuppressive therapy.

102
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:39,520
If you have a patient that receives a transplant and you put them on immunosuppressive therapy,

103
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:44,320
the most common cancer that would then develop in that patient would be squamous cell carcinoma.

104
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:45,200
That's high yield.

105
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:49,560
Arsenic poisoning also increases the risk for squamous cell carcinoma of the skin.

106
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:56,000
We've already talked about that, and chronic inflammation can also increase the risk for squamous cell carcinoma of the skin.

107
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:04,520
For example, if there's a scar from a burn or there's a draining sinus tract, these can also increase the risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the skin.

108
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:12,400
Squamous cell carcinoma presents as an ulcerated nodular mass, usually on the face and classically involving the lower lip.

109
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:21,160
Now remember that the upper lip is involved by basal cell carcinoma classically, and the lower lip is involved by squamous cell carcinoma classically.

110
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:28,400
And I don't know, one of the students told me that he hates memorizing things and that this is a bunch of BS, so BS on top and bottom.

111
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:33,160
But anyway, basal cell on the upper lip and squamous cell carcinoma on the lower lip.

112
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:37,000
The treatment is excision, and metastasis would be uncommon.

113
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,760
It has an excellent prognosis.

114
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:43,760
Actinic keratosis is a precursor to squamous cell carcinoma.

115
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:53,760
It presents as a hyperkeratotic scaly plaque, so you have extra keratin, that's why it's called actinic keratosis, usually on the face, back, or neck.

116
00:06:53,760 --> 00:07:01,760
And it's a precursor to squamous cell carcinoma, and it usually doesn't, but it can progress to the development of squamous cell carcinoma.

117
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:05,760
So really I would say that this is a dysplasia of the squamous cells.

118
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:11,760
Keratoacanthoma is a very well differentiated squamous cell carcinoma.

119
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:16,760
Now what you need to know for the purposes of board exams is that this tumor develops very rapidly,

120
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:22,760
basically within a period of weeks, and then regresses just as quickly, so also within weeks.

121
00:07:22,760 --> 00:07:27,760
And it presents as a cup-shaped tumor with keratin debris in the center.

122
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:35,760
So if this is the surface of the skin, you would see this tumor that looks like this with all this keratin debris in the center.

123
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:38,760
And again, that's a keratoacanthoma.

124
00:07:38,760 --> 00:07:41,760
And that concludes our discussion of epithelial tumors.

125
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:42,760
.

